{"name":"Strawflower","hex":"#ddbdba","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"strawflower","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any of many Australian plants of the genus Xerochrysum, especially Xerochrysum bracteatum, having deep yellow flowers than can be readily dried."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/strawflower"}],"entries":[{"title":"Xerochrysum bracteatum","blocks":[{"text":"Xerochrysum bracteatum, commonly known as the golden everlasting or strawflower, is a flowering plant in the family Asteraceae native to Australia. Described by Étienne Pierre Ventenat in 1803, it was known as Helichrysum bracteatum for many years before being transferred to a new genus Xerochrysum in 1990. It is an annual up to 1 m (3.3 ft) tall with green or grey leafy foliage. Golden yellow or white flower heads are produced from spring to autumn; their distinctive feature is the papery bracts that resemble petals. The species is widespread, growing in a variety of habitats across the country, from rainforest margins to deserts and subalpine areas. The golden everlasting serves as food for various larvae of lepidopterans, and adult butterflies, hoverflies, native bees, small beetles, and grasshoppers visit the flower heads."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/26/Xerochrysum_bracteatum_on_a_dune.jpg/330px-Xerochrysum_bracteatum_on_a_dune.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Xerochrysum_bracteatum","wikidata":"Q159089"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/93/Xerochrysum_bracteatum_head9_-_Flickr_-_Macleay_Grass_Man.jpg/500px-Xerochrysum_bracteatum_head9_-_Flickr_-_Macleay_Grass_Man.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/93/Xerochrysum_bracteatum_head9_-_Flickr_-_Macleay_Grass_Man.jpg/1280px-Xerochrysum_bracteatum_